WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:04.967
(shrill electronic music)

00:00:06.679 --> 00:00:08.159
[Narrator] Conventional wisdom these days

00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:10.599
says that robots will soon put millions

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:13.097
of factory workers out of jobs.

00:00:13.097 --> 00:00:16.465
(playful electronic music)

00:00:16.465 --> 00:00:20.444
This may sound like the
fever dream of Dr. Evil,

00:00:20.444 --> 00:00:22.844
but in fact, there are
very pleasant people

00:00:22.844 --> 00:00:24.427
making this happen.

00:00:25.752 --> 00:00:28.512
May I introduce Suzanne Gildert?

00:00:28.512 --> 00:00:30.804
A budding AI overlord and founder

00:00:30.804 --> 00:00:33.902
of Toronto startup Kindred AI.

00:00:33.902 --> 00:00:37.485
(playful electronic music)

00:00:44.266 --> 00:00:45.986
Tell me about these guys.

00:00:45.986 --> 00:00:49.154
So these are research prototypes.

00:00:49.154 --> 00:00:52.329
So they're some of the first
robots we built at Kindred.

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:53.786
(mechanical sounds)

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:55.535
We tend to work with small robots.

00:00:55.535 --> 00:00:59.115
It's a bit like if you
imagine a child growing up,

00:00:59.115 --> 00:01:00.325
and it breaks a lot of things.

00:01:00.325 --> 00:01:02.213
Now imagine if the child was six feet tall

00:01:02.213 --> 00:01:04.772
when it had the brain of a six month old.

00:01:04.772 --> 00:01:07.984
It would be terribly dangerous.

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:10.532
How many of these robots
have ever slapped you?

00:01:10.532 --> 00:01:14.092
I have been hit in the face
by robots a couple of times.

00:01:14.092 --> 00:01:16.062
(laughs)

00:01:16.062 --> 00:01:18.513
Suzanne seems nice enough.

00:01:18.513 --> 00:01:21.899
(upbeat electronic music)

00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:24.232
She makes exotic digital art

00:01:26.499 --> 00:01:28.539
and she loves cats to the
point where she's built

00:01:28.539 --> 00:01:31.927
a robotic fleet of them for the office.

00:01:31.927 --> 00:01:36.160
This one, I believe, is called Pinkfoot.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:40.087
It's a quadruped robot loosely
based on a cat anatomy,

00:01:40.087 --> 00:01:45.087
although it's not a very highly
faithful representation yet.

00:01:45.647 --> 00:01:48.419
When you were growing up you
would build things as well?

00:01:48.419 --> 00:01:50.836
Yeah, that's correct. Yeah.

00:01:51.876 --> 00:01:56.876
So I was really enthralled by
electronics at an early age.

00:01:56.957 --> 00:01:58.048
I guess most little girls would be

00:01:58.048 --> 00:01:59.997
looking at trays of beads and things,

00:01:59.997 --> 00:02:01.699
and I was looking at trays of resistors

00:02:01.699 --> 00:02:03.557
and capacitors and little components,

00:02:03.557 --> 00:02:07.307
but having the same kind
of reaction to them.

00:02:08.637 --> 00:02:09.470
But don't be fooled

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:14.077
by the hobby electronics
and the cute cat bots.

00:02:14.077 --> 00:02:16.744
Suzanne is a keen businesswoman.

00:02:18.749 --> 00:02:20.048
And Kindred has recently embarked

00:02:20.048 --> 00:02:23.157
on its first commercial venture.

00:02:23.157 --> 00:02:24.804
(mechanical whirring)

00:02:24.804 --> 00:02:27.592
What's going on here is that
we have a bank of robots

00:02:27.592 --> 00:02:31.173
that are learning, so they
are continuously running,

00:02:31.173 --> 00:02:33.002
picking up objects.

00:02:33.002 --> 00:02:34.449
These would run all day?

00:02:34.449 --> 00:02:36.609
All day, all night.

00:02:36.609 --> 00:02:40.776
("Dance of the Sugar Plum Fairy")

00:02:41.972 --> 00:02:44.218
Powered by state of the art AI,

00:02:44.218 --> 00:02:47.420
these arms can do something
that's very easy for a human,

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.071
but very hard for a bot.

00:02:50.071 --> 00:02:54.571
Pick up objects of different
shapes and put them down.

00:02:55.412 --> 00:02:56.761
Most factories still use people

00:02:56.761 --> 00:03:01.041
to do that sort of thing,
lots and lots of people.

00:03:01.041 --> 00:03:02.652
Today everyone's shopping on e-commerce,

00:03:02.652 --> 00:03:05.172
thousands and thousands of
different types of objects,

00:03:05.172 --> 00:03:07.841
shapes, textures, weights.

00:03:07.841 --> 00:03:09.721
How do you pick that up?

00:03:09.721 --> 00:03:11.492
Right now it's human, so
you have millions of humans

00:03:11.492 --> 00:03:12.940
in warehouses just picking up things

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:15.180
and putting it into another location.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.620
So we're teaching our
robots how to do that.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:20.756
(mechanical whirring sound)

00:03:20.756 --> 00:03:23.047
What's the hard part, is it
figuring out what's a belt?

00:03:23.047 --> 00:03:23.989
What's a shirt?

00:03:23.989 --> 00:03:25.597
Or it's just, how to grasp it?

00:03:25.597 --> 00:03:26.430
Yeah, exactly.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:27.263
It's very hard to pick it up, right?

00:03:27.263 --> 00:03:28.597
So things will show up
in any shape, right?

00:03:28.597 --> 00:03:29.887
And you gotta figure out how to pick it up

00:03:29.887 --> 00:03:32.738
without dropping it,
put it in the location,

00:03:32.738 --> 00:03:35.238
so it takes a lot of training.

00:03:36.395 --> 00:03:37.989
Part of that training involves,

00:03:37.989 --> 00:03:39.822
of all things, humans.

00:03:40.677 --> 00:03:44.269
Robot pilots who manually
control the arms while the AI

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:48.269
watches and learns the
finer points of grabbing.

00:03:49.566 --> 00:03:50.658
Alright man, teach me
how to use this thing.

00:03:50.658 --> 00:03:52.575
Alright, have a seat.

00:03:54.687 --> 00:03:57.029
So you see a 3D mouse here,
this lets you navigate

00:03:57.029 --> 00:03:59.159
the arm through three-dimensional space.

00:03:59.159 --> 00:04:02.098
So imagine you're holding
the arm in that left hand

00:04:02.098 --> 00:04:05.418
and you're just moving it around.

00:04:05.418 --> 00:04:07.759
Move it slowly, gently.

00:04:07.759 --> 00:04:08.842
There you go.

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:13.895
Trying to get the Oreos, I gotta go up.

00:04:13.895 --> 00:04:16.853
Oh shoot, I went too far up.

00:04:16.853 --> 00:04:18.436
I want these Oreos.

00:04:19.437 --> 00:04:20.763
(Host groans)

00:04:20.763 --> 00:04:22.215
(Host and George laugh)

00:04:22.215 --> 00:04:23.512
You lose.

00:04:23.512 --> 00:04:25.627
(Host and George laugh)

00:04:25.627 --> 00:04:28.127
Come back to me, arm.

00:04:29.408 --> 00:04:30.658
There. Success.

00:04:31.736 --> 00:04:34.604
(playful electronic music)

00:04:34.604 --> 00:04:35.517
It's like being at an arcade.

00:04:35.517 --> 00:04:36.757
Basically.

00:04:36.757 --> 00:04:37.590
But you actually get to win something.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:38.423
Exactly.

00:04:38.423 --> 00:04:41.932
(Host and George laugh)

00:04:41.932 --> 00:04:44.022
Just down the hall, Kindred keeps a room

00:04:44.022 --> 00:04:47.313
full of pilots doing the same thing as me.

00:04:47.313 --> 00:04:50.721
Only these guys are actually competent.

00:04:50.721 --> 00:04:53.681
They're remotely overseeing
some arms in a Gap factory

00:04:53.681 --> 00:04:56.181
1,000 miles away in Tennessee.

00:04:59.593 --> 00:05:01.201
How long have you been a robot pilot?

00:05:01.201 --> 00:05:03.007
Just over a month, actually.

00:05:03.007 --> 00:05:04.788
I've only been here five weeks.

00:05:04.788 --> 00:05:07.296
What was the training process like?

00:05:07.296 --> 00:05:10.529
Almost like playing a video game.

00:05:10.529 --> 00:05:12.081
It's like a shirt gun?

00:05:12.081 --> 00:05:13.009
That's a backpack.

00:05:13.009 --> 00:05:13.991
That's a backpack, okay.

00:05:13.991 --> 00:05:15.769
(Sai laughs)

00:05:15.769 --> 00:05:17.049
Somebody's undies?

00:05:17.049 --> 00:05:18.231
Oh, there it goes.

00:05:18.231 --> 00:05:20.721
Yeah, one shirt at a time.

00:05:20.721 --> 00:05:23.031
(Host and Sai laugh)

00:05:23.031 --> 00:05:26.614
(mysterious chiming music)

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:32.088
As the arms observe their human guides,

00:05:32.088 --> 00:05:33.918
they gradually learn how to do better

00:05:33.918 --> 00:05:37.085
at picking up T-shirts and shoe boxes.

00:05:38.238 --> 00:05:41.046
Eventually, they'll be fully autonomous

00:05:41.046 --> 00:05:45.150
and Sai's services will
no longer be required.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.699
One day, this is just gonna light up

00:05:46.699 --> 00:05:48.659
and it's going to be picking
the objects all the time.

00:05:48.659 --> 00:05:49.728
Pretty much, pretty much.

00:05:49.728 --> 00:05:52.967
That's the ultimate end
goal, at least for these

00:05:52.967 --> 00:05:55.859
to have it just constantly
whirring and going.

00:05:55.859 --> 00:05:57.686
(robot arm whirring)

00:05:57.686 --> 00:05:58.968
And the people will be free.

00:05:58.968 --> 00:06:01.360
The people will be free to do
other more important things.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:05.150
(Host and Sai laugh)

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:06.568
Sai seemed kind of happy

00:06:06.568 --> 00:06:09.720
about the prospect of unemployment.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:12.637
But I was concerned for his future.

00:06:14.979 --> 00:06:17.446
(mechanical whirring sound)

00:06:17.446 --> 00:06:20.044
Isn't there something grim about the human

00:06:20.044 --> 00:06:22.024
training their end?

00:06:22.024 --> 00:06:25.505
Yeah, it's not good to
take people's jobs away,

00:06:25.505 --> 00:06:28.154
but this kind of technology
coming into the workforce

00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:30.744
should make us start
thinking about how we're

00:06:30.744 --> 00:06:32.886
going to pay people in the future.

00:06:32.886 --> 00:06:37.515
Because AI is not just going
to automate manual labor jobs.

00:06:37.515 --> 00:06:39.675
It's going to automate things like doctors

00:06:39.675 --> 00:06:42.033
and lawyers and accountants very soon.

00:06:42.033 --> 00:06:43.486
So I think there's gonna be issues.

00:06:43.486 --> 00:06:45.033
There's gonna be a lot of disruption

00:06:45.033 --> 00:06:47.533
when these things come online.

00:06:49.217 --> 00:06:51.326
Suzanne is a realist,

00:06:51.326 --> 00:06:53.576
but she's also an optimist.

00:06:57.017 --> 00:06:58.705
In her vision of the future,

00:06:58.705 --> 00:07:01.874
robots won't be mindless
competitors to humanity.

00:07:01.874 --> 00:07:05.353
They'll be full-fledged
citizens like the rest of us.

00:07:05.353 --> 00:07:07.375
One of the crazy ideas
that I see you talk about

00:07:07.375 --> 00:07:11.036
was you've got a robot and
it's working in a factory

00:07:11.036 --> 00:07:13.604
and then it's gotta go,
maybe it gets paid a wage

00:07:13.604 --> 00:07:17.334
and it goes to buy lithium ion
batteries to keep it going.

00:07:17.334 --> 00:07:19.653
Why would that have to happen?

00:07:19.653 --> 00:07:21.204
I mean, through having a physical body,

00:07:21.204 --> 00:07:26.204
they will have a lot of physical
needs just like we have.

00:07:26.495 --> 00:07:28.924
You might have to go to their repair shop

00:07:28.924 --> 00:07:31.094
to get a motor looked at
or something like that

00:07:31.094 --> 00:07:33.014
and they'll have to
pay someone to do that.

00:07:33.014 --> 00:07:34.943
I think they'll just be
contributing to our economy

00:07:34.943 --> 00:07:36.745
in the same way we do.

00:07:36.745 --> 00:07:38.744
(mechanical whirring)

00:07:38.744 --> 00:07:41.116
And if they have brains
like us, they'll want

00:07:41.116 --> 00:07:43.785
to explore new things
they've never seen before.

00:07:43.785 --> 00:07:47.584
They'll want to learn things,
they'll want to perhaps rest

00:07:47.584 --> 00:07:49.676
so that their mind has time to consolidate

00:07:49.676 --> 00:07:51.025
all this new information.

00:07:51.025 --> 00:07:53.289
I keep trying to picture it in my head.

00:07:53.289 --> 00:07:57.582
Does the robot worker go
home and sit on the couch?

00:07:57.582 --> 00:07:59.473
Or watch TV after work?

00:07:59.473 --> 00:08:01.804
I don't see why not,
probably watch cat videos

00:08:01.804 --> 00:08:03.048
like the rest of us.

00:08:03.048 --> 00:08:04.533
(Host and Suzanne laugh)

00:08:04.533 --> 00:08:05.863
It's hard to tell sometimes

00:08:05.863 --> 00:08:09.196
if Suzanne is laughing with us or at us.

00:08:10.514 --> 00:08:13.573
Either way, I plan to clear
a nice spot on the couch

00:08:13.573 --> 00:08:17.674
for my robot friends, oil
up some delicious popcorn,

00:08:17.674 --> 00:08:19.257
and smile politely.

